From b228a2181f72151e499ad781cc93683edcdd5359 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: verkur46 <mohamadrezasahami1999@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:40:44 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 101/230] Make the match page live-accurate: goal scores,
 faces, formation pitch, shot plot
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The timeline was not synced with the clock, and a goal read "Goal — Cole
Palmer" without saying what it did to the match. The header polled every five
minutes while the server's live pulse was refreshing the minute and score
straight from ESPN every 15s, so 20s is now the beat — through the half-time
break too, which used to leave a match frozen on "HT" until the screen was
touched. An SSE goal event now invalidates every match-scoped query family
(timeline, comments, commentary, players, ratings, lineups, predictions), not
just the header, so a goal moves the feed and the scoreboard together. Each
goal carries the score it produced, walked as a prefix count over the
timeline's own goals (src/lib/timelineScore.ts, tested), so a goal reads
"1–0" as it lands.

Player faces: PlayerAvatar renders the ESPN headshot in a circle and falls
back to the player's initials on the same footprint when the feed has no
artwork or the URL 404s, used by both the lineups pitch/bench and the club
page's squad list.

The lineups tab draws a pitch instead of a list of names — turf, real
markings, the XI standing in its published shape with faces and shirt
numbers. That surfaced a placement bug worth calling out: ESPN files wingers
as midfielders, so a published 4-3-3 arrives as five midfielders and one
forward and dealing it by unit silently dropped both wide men off the pitch.
placePublishedXI rebalances those advanced roles into the forward band when
the shape has room, and falls back to one band per unit when the XI does not
fit its own shape — either way every starter is drawn.

The stats tab gains a Pitch View: the match's located events dropped into the
6x4 grid the Shot Predictor is scored against (geometry mirrored from
server/scoring/shotGrid.ts and pinned by tests), and a to-scale pitch with
each shot drawn at its real position and, for a goal, the line the ball took
to the goal mouth. It refreshes on the same query the timeline owns, so it
moves live. ESPN publishes no pass coordinates at all, so nothing here
invents them: the panel draws what the feed locates and says so when it
locates nothing.

